<h2 id="s1">1. Understanding Email Header Structure</h2>

<p>
  Every email you receive carries a hidden story — a full digital passport stamped at each border crossing as the message travels from sender to inbox. This record is the <strong>email header</strong>. To the naked eye it looks like a wall of cryptic text, but to a cybersecurity investigator it is a detailed evidence trail revealing who sent the message, from where, through which servers, and whether anyone tampered with it along the way.
</p>

<p>
  Think of an email like a physical letter. The envelope carries the postmarks from every post office it passed through. The header is that envelope — the body of the email is just what's inside. Most email clients hide the header by default to keep the interface clean, but investigators can always access it through "View Source", "Show Original", or similar menu options.
</p>

<h3>What Does a Full Header Look Like?</h3>

<p>A raw email header contains dozens of fields. Here is a simplified but realistic example:</p>

<pre><code>Delivered-To: victim@example.com
Received: by 2002:a05:6000:abc with SMTP id xyz; Fri, 10 May 2024 09:12:33 -0700
Received: from mail.suspiciousdomain.net (mail.suspiciousdomain.net [203.0.113.42])
        by mx.google.com with ESMTP id abc123
        for &lt;victim@example.com&gt;; Fri, 10 May 2024 09:12:32 -0700
Authentication-Results: mx.google.com;
       dkim=fail header.i=@suspiciousdomain.net;
       spf=softfail (google.com: domain of admin@suspiciousdomain.net
           does not designate 203.0.113.42 as permitted sender);
       dmarc=fail (p=NONE) header.from=suspiciousdomain.net
From: "PayPal Security" &lt;admin@suspiciousdomain.net&gt;
To: victim@example.com
Subject: Urgent: Your account has been suspended
Date: Fri, 10 May 2024 16:12:28 +0000
Message-ID: &lt;CAGxxxx@mail.suspiciousdomain.net&gt;
MIME-Version: 1.0
X-Mailer: PHPMailer 6.4.0
Content-Type: text/html; charset=UTF-8</code></pre>

<p>This single block of text tells us the recipient, the route the email took, authentication results, and even the software used to send it. Let's break each piece down systematically.</p>

<h3>Key Header Fields Explained</h3>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Field</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Investigative Value</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>Received</code></td><td>Logged by each mail server the email passed through</td><td>Reveals the actual origin IP and path</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>From</code></td><td>The display name and address shown to the recipient</td><td>Easily spoofed — do not trust blindly</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>Return-Path</code></td><td>Where bounce messages go; set by the sending server</td><td>Often differs from <code>From</code> in phishing emails</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>Message-ID</code></td><td>Unique identifier for the email</td><td>Domain in Message-ID should match sending domain</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>X-Originating-IP</code></td><td>The original client IP, sometimes added by webmail</td><td>Can directly expose the attacker's IP</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>Authentication-Results</code></td><td>Summary of SPF, DKIM, DMARC checks by receiving server</td><td>Core forensic evidence field</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>X-Mailer</code></td><td>Email software used to send the message</td><td>Exposes bulk senders, phishing kits (e.g. PHPMailer)</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>Content-Type</code></td><td>MIME type of the email body</td><td>HTML-only emails are a phishing red flag</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<div class="callout">
  <span class="callout-title">📌 Investigator's Note</span>
  The <code>From</code> header is what your email client displays in the "From:" field. It is trivially easy to forge. Always look at <code>Return-Path</code>, <code>Received</code> chains, and authentication results instead of trusting the display name or address.
</div>

