<p>Each server that processes the email stamps a <code>Received</code> header at the <strong>top</strong> of the existing headers. This means headers are read <em>bottom-up</em> chronologically — the bottom-most <code>Received</code> line is the first server the email touched, and the top-most is the last.</p>

<!-- SECTION 2 -->
<h2 id="s2">2. The Received Chain — Tracing the Journey</h2>

<p>
  The <code>Received</code> chain is the most important forensic element in an email header. Think of it as a chain of custody document — each server in the path adds its own notarized entry. But unlike a legal document, these entries can be partially forged by the sending party. Understanding which parts to trust (and which to be skeptical of) is the essence of header analysis.
</p>

<h3>How to Read a Received Header</h3>

<p>Each <code>Received</code> line follows a rough pattern:</p>

<pre><code>Received: from [sending server hostname] ([sending server IP])
          by [receiving server hostname]
          with [protocol]
          id [message identifier]
          for &lt;recipient@domain.com&gt;;
          [timestamp with timezone]</code></pre>

<p>A real example:</p>

<pre><code>Received: from mail.attackerdomain.net (mail.attackerdomain.net [203.0.113.42])
          by mx.victim-company.com with ESMTP id z12abc
          for &lt;cfo@victim-company.com&gt;;
          Mon, 13 May 2024 14:23:10 +0000</code></pre>

<div class="callout warning">
  <span class="callout-title">⚠️ Critical Rule</span>
  <strong>Never trust Received headers added by the sender's own infrastructure.</strong> The bottom one or two Received lines may have been injected by the attacker to create a false sense of legitimacy. The <strong>first trustworthy Received line</strong> is the one added by your organisation's own mail gateway — because that server is under your control and cannot be faked by the sender.
</div>

<h3>The Golden Rule: Read Bottom-Up</h3>

<div class="steps">
  <div class="step">
    <div class="step-content">
      <strong>Locate the bottom-most Received header</strong>
      <p>This is where the email started. It may contain the actual sender's mail server or, if they're hiding behind a relay, the first hop after origin.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step">
    <div class="step-content">
      <strong>Work upward, identifying each hop</strong>
      <p>Each subsequent Received line should make geographic and logical sense. A message going from Nigeria to a US relay to a UK inbox has a plausible path. A message appearing to jump from Google to Gmail without explanation may have fabricated headers.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step">
    <div class="step-content">
      <strong>Identify the first server you control</strong>
      <p>This is your "trust anchor." The IP address in the Received line immediately <em>below</em> your gateway's entry is the last external server — and therefore the most reliable source of the originating IP.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step">
    <div class="step-content">
      <strong>Check for time inconsistencies</strong>
      <p>Timestamps on Received headers should increase as you read upward. If timestamps go backwards or have large unexplained gaps, this is a red flag for header manipulation.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<h3>Legitimate vs Suspicious Received Chains</h3>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Indicator</th><th>Legitimate Email</th><th>Suspicious Email</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>Number of hops</td>
      <td>2–4 servers</td>
      <td>1 (injected), or 8+ (obfuscating origin)</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Hostname resolution</td>
      <td>IP resolves to legitimate hostname</td>
      <td>IP is an open relay, VPS, or doesn't resolve</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Timestamp flow</td>
      <td>Consistent, increasing upward</td>
      <td>Out of order, same-second entries, or large gaps</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Protocol used</td>
      <td>ESMTP with TLS encryption</td>
      <td>Plain SMTP with no encryption</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<!-- SECTION 3 -->
<h2 id="s3">3. SPF — Sender Policy Framework</h2>

<p>
  Imagine you work at a company and someone walks into your building claiming to be from a well-known law firm. Before trusting them, you'd call the law firm and ask, "Is this person authorised to speak on your behalf?" <strong>SPF is exactly that phone call</strong> — but for email servers.
</p>

<p>
  SPF is a DNS-based authentication mechanism that allows a domain owner to publish a list of all IP addresses authorised to send email on their behalf. When a receiving mail server gets an email claiming to be from <code>paypal.com</code>, it queries the DNS for PayPal's SPF record and checks whether the sending IP is on the approved list.
</p>

<h3>How SPF Works — Step by Step</h3>

