    <text x="545" y="121" text-anchor="middle" fill="#f4a0b0" font-family="JetBrains Mono" font-size="11" font-weight="700">SPF FAIL</text>
    <text x="545" y="138" text-anchor="middle" fill="#b0c8e0" font-family="JetBrains Mono" font-size="8">IP not authorised</text>
    <!-- Bottom text -->
    <text x="265" y="190" text-anchor="middle" fill="#4a6a88" font-family="JetBrains Mono" font-size="9">203.0.113.42 is NOT in PayPal's SPF record</text>
    <text x="265" y="205" text-anchor="middle" fill="#c05070" font-family="JetBrains Mono" font-size="9">→ Email flagged as spoofed / failed SPF</text>
  </svg>
</div>

<h3>Understanding SPF DNS Records</h3>

<p>An SPF record lives in a domain's DNS as a TXT record. Here's how to read one:</p>

<pre><code>v=spf1 ip4:192.0.2.0/24 include:_spf.google.com include:sendgrid.net -all

v=spf1          → SPF version 1 (always this)
ip4:192.0.2.0/24→ This entire subnet is authorised to send
include:        → Also check another domain's SPF record
-all            → Any IP not listed above = HARD FAIL (reject)
~all            → Any IP not listed above = SOFT FAIL (accept but mark)
?all            → Neutral (no policy)
+all            → ANY IP can send (dangerous! avoid)
</code></pre>

<h3>SPF Result Codes and Their Meaning</h3>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Result</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Typical Action</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><span class="tag">pass</span></td><td>IP is authorised by SPF record</td><td>Accept</td></tr>
    <tr><td><span class="tag">fail</span></td><td>IP is explicitly NOT authorised</td><td>Reject</td></tr>
    <tr><td><span class="tag">softfail</span></td><td>IP is not authorised but policy is lenient</td><td>Accept with warning / mark as spam</td></tr>
    <tr><td><span class="tag">neutral</span></td><td>Domain owner makes no assertion</td><td>Accept</td></tr>
    <tr><td><span class="tag">none</span></td><td>Domain has no SPF record</td><td>Accept (can't verify)</td></tr>
    <tr><td><span class="tag">permerror</span></td><td>SPF record is malformed</td><td>Varies by policy</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<div class="callout warning">
  <span class="callout-title">⚠️ SPF Limitation</span>
  SPF only checks the <strong>envelope sender</strong> (the <code>MAIL FROM</code> address used during SMTP negotiation), <em>not</em> the <code>From:</code> header that users see. An attacker can pass SPF while still displaying a fake friendly name in the visible <code>From:</code> field. This is why DKIM and DMARC are also needed.
</div>

<!-- SECTION 4 -->
<h2 id="s4">4. DKIM — DomainKeys Identified Mail</h2>

<p>
  SPF tells you "is this email from an authorised server?" DKIM answers a different but equally important question: <strong>"Has this email been tampered with in transit?"</strong>
</p>

<p>
  DKIM uses <strong>public-key cryptography</strong>. The sending mail server signs the email with a private key (kept secret). The matching public key is published in DNS. Any receiving server can look up the public key and verify the signature. If the email was altered even a single character after signing, the signature breaks.
</p>

<h3>How DKIM Works — The Signing Process</h3>

<div class="steps">
  <div class="step">
    <div class="step-content">
      <strong>The sender's mail server selects headers and body to sign</strong>
      <p>Typically includes <code>From</code>, <code>To</code>, <code>Subject</code>, <code>Date</code>, and the body. The selection is recorded in the DKIM header as <code>h=</code> tags.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step">
    <div class="step-content">
      <strong>A cryptographic hash is generated and signed with the private key</strong>
      <p>This produces a digital signature — a unique fingerprint tied to both the content and the sender's identity. Changing even one character invalidates the signature.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step">
    <div class="step-content">
      <strong>The signature is added to the email as a DKIM-Signature header</strong>
      <p>This header travels with the email and contains all information needed to verify it.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step">
    <div class="step-content">
      <strong>The receiving server fetches the public key from DNS</strong>
      <p>Using the domain (<code>d=</code>) and selector (<code>s=</code>) fields in the DKIM header, it queries <code>selector._domainkey.domain.com</code>.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step">
    <div class="step-content">
      <strong>The signature is verified</strong>
      <p>If it matches, DKIM passes. If the email was modified in transit or the sender doesn't control the domain they claim, DKIM fails.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<h3>Anatomy of a DKIM-Signature Header</h3>

<pre><code>DKIM-Signature: v=1; a=rsa-sha256; c=relaxed/relaxed;
        d=example.com;            ← The signing domain
        s=20230601;               ← The key selector
        h=from:to:subject:date;   ← Headers that were signed
        bh=abc123xyz...;          ← Hash of the email body
        b=DKIM_SIGNATURE_HERE...  ← The actual cryptographic signature

v=1           → DKIM version 1
a=rsa-sha256  → Algorithm used (RSA with SHA-256)
c=relaxed/... → Canonicalization (how whitespace is normalised)
d=            → Domain responsible for the signature
s=            → Selector to look up public key in DNS
h=            → List of signed headers
bh=           → Body hash
b=            → The signature itself (base64 encoded)
</code></pre>

<div class="callout">
  <span class="callout-title">🔍 Investigator Tip</span>
  When DKIM passes, it means the email was sent by someone with access to the private key for that domain. When DKIM fails, the email was either tampered with, sent by someone who doesn't control the domain, or the domain's DNS records changed after sending. A <strong>DKIM pass from a domain other than the sender's</strong> (called a <em>third-party signature</em>) is common in legitimate bulk email but should be noted.
</div>

<h3>Common DKIM Failure Scenarios</h3>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Forwarded emails:</strong> Many mail forwarders modify the email body or headers, breaking the DKIM signature. This is a known limitation, not always a sign of attack.</li>
  <li><strong>Mailing lists:</strong> Lists often add footers or change subject lines, invalidating signatures.</li>
  <li><strong>Phishing:</strong> An attacker claiming to be <code>bank.com</code> won't have the private key for <code>bank.com</code>, so DKIM will fail.</li>
  <li><strong>Key rotation:</strong> If the sending domain deleted an old selector, previously signed emails will now fail to verify.</li>
</ul>

<!-- SECTION 5 -->
<h2 id="s5">5. DMARC — Domain-based Message Authentication, Reporting &amp; Conformance</h2>

<p>
  SPF and DKIM individually have gaps. SPF doesn't protect the visible <code>From:</code> address. DKIM doesn't prevent replay attacks using old signed emails. <strong>DMARC bridges these gaps</strong> by requiring that either SPF or DKIM (or both) not only pass, but <em>align</em> with the domain in the visible <code>From:</code> header.
</p>

<p>
  Think of DMARC as the manager who checks that your ID not only says you're a valid person (SPF/DKIM) but also that the name on the ID matches the name on your booking (the <code>From:</code> address).
</p>

<h3>DMARC Policy Options</h3>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Policy (<code>p=</code>)</th><th>What Happens on Failure</th><th>Typical Use</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>none</code></td><td>No action; reports are sent if <code>rua</code> is set</td><td>Monitoring mode for new deployments</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>quarantine</code></td><td>Email delivered to spam/junk folder</td><td>Transitioning to enforcement</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>reject</code></td><td>Email rejected at the SMTP level</td><td>Full protection; blocks spoofed email</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h3>Reading a DMARC DNS Record</h3>

<pre><code>v=DMARC1; p=reject; rua=mailto:reports@example.com;
ruf=mailto:forensics@example.com; pct=100; sp=reject;
adkim=s; aspf=s

v=DMARC1         → Version (always DMARC1)
p=reject         → Policy: reject failing emails
rua=mailto:...   → Aggregate reports sent to this address
ruf=mailto:...   → Forensic (failure) reports sent here
pct=100          → Apply policy to 100% of emails
sp=reject        → Same policy for subdomains
adkim=s          → Strict DKIM alignment required
aspf=s           → Strict SPF alignment required
</code></pre>

<h3>DMARC Alignment — The Missing Piece</h3>

<div class="callout success">
  <span class="callout-title">✅ What Alignment Means</span>
  <strong>SPF Alignment:</strong> The domain in the <code>Return-Path</code> (envelope sender) must match the <code>From:</code> domain.<br><br>
  <strong>DKIM Alignment:</strong> The <code>d=</code> domain in the DKIM signature must match the <code>From:</code> domain.<br><br>
  Without alignment, an attacker could legitimately send email via their own server (passing SPF for their domain) while displaying your brand name in the From header. DMARC alignment prevents this.
</div>

