<h2 id="s6">6. Tracing the Originating IP &amp; Blacklist Checks</h2>

<p>
  Once you've identified the <strong>first trustworthy Received header</strong> (the one your mail gateway added), the IP address in the line immediately below it is your prime suspect — the last external server that handed your mail server the message.
</p>

<h3>Step-by-Step: Finding the True Originating IP</h3>

<div class="steps">
  <div class="step">
    <div class="step-content">
      <strong>Open the full email headers</strong>
      <p>In Gmail: three-dot menu → "Show original". In Outlook: File → Properties → Internet Headers. In Apple Mail: View → Message → All Headers.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step">
    <div class="step-content">
      <strong>Find all Received headers and list them</strong>
      <p>Read from bottom to top. The bottom-most entry is the true start of the journey.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step">
    <div class="step-content">
      <strong>Identify your organisation's mail gateway</strong>
      <p>Look for your own domain (e.g., <code>mx.yourcompany.com</code>) in the Received headers. This is your trust anchor.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step">
    <div class="step-content">
      <strong>Extract the IP from the Received line just below your gateway</strong>
      <p>It appears in square brackets, e.g., <code>[203.0.113.42]</code>. This is the IP you will investigate.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step">
    <div class="step-content">
      <strong>Run a reverse DNS (rDNS) lookup</strong>
      <p>Use <code>nslookup 203.0.113.42</code> or <code>dig -x 203.0.113.42</code>. A legitimate IP resolves to a hostname matching its sending domain. A result like <code>203-0-113-42.static.vps-host.net</code> is a red flag.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step">
    <div class="step-content">
      <strong>Check the IP against threat intelligence blacklists</strong>
      <p>Query services like Spamhaus, Barracuda Central, MXToolbox, or VirusTotal. Blacklisted IPs are known spam sources or command-and-control servers.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<h3>Popular Blacklist Lookup Tools</h3>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Tool</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Best For</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>MXToolbox Blacklist</td><td>Checks 100+ DNSBLs simultaneously</td><td>Quick first-pass blacklist screening</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Spamhaus ZEN</td><td>Combines multiple Spamhaus lists</td><td>Authoritative spam IP database</td></tr>
    <tr><td>VirusTotal</td><td>Checks IPs and domains against 70+ engines</td><td>Comprehensive threat intelligence</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Barracuda Central</td><td>Checks Barracuda's reputation database</td><td>Mail-focused reputation scoring</td></tr>
    <tr><td>AbuseIPDB</td><td>Community-reported malicious IPs</td><td>Recent attack reports and categories</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Talos Intelligence</td><td>Cisco's IP/domain reputation service</td><td>Enterprise threat intelligence</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h3>Example Blacklist Query (Command Line)</h3>

<pre><code># Reverse the IP octets and append the DNSBL zone
# For IP 203.0.113.42 → query 42.113.0.203.zen.spamhaus.org

nslookup 42.113.0.203.zen.spamhaus.org

# If you get back a 127.0.0.x result → IP is LISTED (blacklisted)
# If you get NXDOMAIN → IP is NOT listed (clean)

# Response codes for Spamhaus ZEN:
# 127.0.0.2 → SBL (Spamhaus Block List — confirmed spam source)
# 127.0.0.4 → XBL (Exploits Block List — compromised/malware host)
# 127.0.0.10 → PBL (Policy Block List — dynamic IP, shouldn't send email)
</code></pre>

<!-- SECTION 7 -->
<h2 id="s7">7. WHOIS, GeoIP, and Organisational Mapping</h2>

<p>
  An IP address by itself tells you nothing about who is behind it. To transform a raw number like <code>203.0.113.42</code> into actionable intelligence, you use two complementary tools: <strong>WHOIS</strong> (for registration and ownership data) and <strong>GeoIP</strong> (for geographic location estimates).
</p>

<h3>WHOIS Lookups</h3>

<p>
  WHOIS is a protocol that queries public registration databases maintained by Regional Internet Registries (RIRs). Every IP block is registered to an organisation, and WHOIS exposes that registration data.
</p>

<p>The five RIRs covering the world:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>ARIN</strong> — North America</li>
  <li><strong>RIPE NCC</strong> — Europe, Middle East, Central Asia</li>
  <li><strong>APNIC</strong> — Asia-Pacific</li>
  <li><strong>LACNIC</strong> — Latin America and Caribbean</li>
  <li><strong>AFRINIC</strong> — Africa</li>
</ul>

<pre><code># Running a WHOIS lookup in terminal:
whois 203.0.113.42

# Key fields to look for in the output:
OrgName:        Some Hosting Provider LLC
OrgId:          SHPL-123
Address:        100 Server Street
City:           Amsterdam
Country:        NL
NetRange:       203.0.113.0 - 203.0.113.255
CIDR:           203.0.113.0/24
NetType:        Direct Allocation
Comment:        Abuse reports: abuse@shpl.net
RegDate:        2019-03-15
</code></pre>

<div class="callout">
  <span class="callout-title">🔍 What to Look For</span>
  <strong>Hosting providers</strong> (DigitalOcean, OVH, Hetzner, Vultr) are common origins for phishing emails because attackers rent cheap VPS servers. <strong>Residential ISPs</strong> (Comcast, BT) suggest a compromised home computer (bot). <strong>Cloud providers</strong> (AWS, Azure, GCP) may indicate a compromised cloud instance. The abuse contact email is essential for incident reporting.
</div>

<h3>GeoIP Mapping</h3>

<p>
  GeoIP services map an IP address to an approximate physical location. They work by combining WHOIS registration data, BGP routing tables, latency measurements, and crowdsourced corrections.
</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>GeoIP Tool</th><th>Free Tier</th><th>Accuracy Level</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>MaxMind GeoIP2</td><td>GeoLite2 (free)</td><td>Country: ~99%, City: ~70%</td></tr>
    <tr><td>IPinfo.io</td><td>50k requests/month</td><td>Country + ISP excellent</td></tr>
    <tr><td>ip-api.com</td><td>45 requests/minute</td><td>Good for quick lookups</td></tr>
    <tr><td>ipgeolocation.io</td><td>1000/day free</td><td>City + timezone data</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h3>Limitations of GeoIP Evidence</h3>

<div class="callout warning">
  <span class="callout-title">⚠️ Critical Limitations</span>
  GeoIP data should <strong>never be treated as definitive proof</strong> of an attacker's physical location in legal or investigative contexts. Key limitations:
  <ul style="margin-top:10px;">
    <li><strong>VPNs and proxies</strong> route traffic through a different country entirely. A geolocation in the Netherlands may simply mean the attacker used a Dutch VPN node.</li>
    <li><strong>Tor exit nodes</strong> make the traffic appear to originate from the exit node's location.</li>
    <li><strong>Cloud instances</strong> may be registered in one country but physically hosted in another data centre.</li>
    <li><strong>IP delegation</strong> can place a regional office's IP in the wrong geographic database entry.</li>
    <li><strong>Database staleness</strong> — GeoIP databases are updated regularly but not in real-time. IP reassignments can lag by weeks.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<h3>Building an IP Intelligence Report</h3>

<p>A thorough investigator compiles the following from WHOIS + GeoIP + blacklist checks:</p>

<pre><code>IP INTELLIGENCE REPORT
═══════════════════════════════════════════
IP Address:       203.0.113.42
rDNS:             vps-203-0-113-42.shpl.net
WHOIS Owner:      Some Hosting Provider LLC
RIR:              RIPE NCC
Country (WHOIS):  Netherlands
City (GeoIP):     Amsterdam (~75% confidence)
ASN:              AS12345 (SHPL-AS)
IP Type:          Hosting / Data Centre
Blacklist Status: LISTED in Spamhaus SBL
                  LISTED in AbuseIPDB (25 reports)
Abuse Contact:    abuse@shpl.net
Threat Category:  Spam source, phishing infrastructure
Notes:            VPS rental; attacker's actual location unknown
═══════════════════════════════════════════</code></pre>

