<h2 id="s8">8. Header Spoofing and Phishing Detection</h2>

<p>
  Email header spoofing is the art of making an email appear to come from a trusted source when it does not. This is the foundational technique behind <strong>phishing</strong>, <strong>business email compromise (BEC)</strong>, and <strong>spear-phishing</strong> attacks. Understanding how it works makes you dramatically better at detecting it.
</p>

<h3>Types of Email Spoofing</h3>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Technique</th><th>How It Works</th><th>What the Victim Sees</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>Display Name Spoofing</td>
      <td>Sets <code>From:</code> to "PayPal Security" but with attacker's real email</td>
      <td>"PayPal Security" in the From field</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Exact Domain Spoofing</td>
      <td>Forges the <code>From:</code> domain to match the target brand exactly</td>
      <td>admin@paypal.com (but email fails SPF/DKIM)</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Look-alike Domain</td>
      <td>Registers paypa1.com, paypal-security.com, etc.</td>
      <td>Appears similar at a glance</td></tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Subdomain Spoofing</td>
      <td>Uses security.paypal.attacker.com (attacker controls .attacker.com)</td>
      <td>Shows "paypal" in the domain string</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Unicode Homograph</td>
      <td>Uses visually identical Unicode characters (e.g., Cyrillic "а" vs Latin "a")</td>
      <td>Indistinguishable in many fonts</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Header Injection</td>
      <td>Injects fake Received headers to create a false trail</td>
      <td>Header chain appears legitimate at first glance</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h3>Case Study: A Typical Phishing Email Dissected</h3>

<p>Let's walk through a realistic phishing scenario — a fake "bank security alert" email.</p>

<pre><code>Return-Path: &lt;bounce@spammer-infrastructure.com&gt;     ← Real bounce address
Received: from mx.victim-bank-customers.com (...)     ← INJECTED (fake)
Received: from mail.spammer-infrastructure.com        ← Real sending server
  (mail.spammer-infrastructure.com [198.51.100.77])   ← Real IP
  by mx.targetbank-customers.com with ESMTP id abc;   ← Your gateway (trust anchor)
Authentication-Results: mx.targetbank-customers.com;
  dkim=fail;                                           ← DKIM FAILED
  spf=fail (domain of notices@secure-targetbank.com   ← SPF FAILED
    does not designate 198.51.100.77 as permitted);
  dmarc=fail (p=REJECT)                               ← DMARC FAILED

From: "Target Bank Security" &lt;notices@secure-targetbank.com&gt; ← SPOOFED
To: victim@customer.com
Subject: URGENT: Your account has been compromised - Verify immediately
Date: Mon, 13 May 2024 03:42:11 +0300
X-Mailer: PHPMailer 6.5.1 (https://github.com/PHPMailer/PHPMailer)
                                                      ← Reveals phishing kit</code></pre>

<h4>Red Flags in This Example</h4>

<ul>
  <li><strong>SPF, DKIM, and DMARC all fail</strong> — the trifecta of authentication failure is the clearest indicator of spoofing</li>
  <li><strong>Return-Path doesn't match From</strong> — bounce goes to <code>spammer-infrastructure.com</code> while From claims to be the bank</li>
  <li><strong>Injected Received header</strong> — the first Received line mentions the victim's own gateway as if the email originated there (impossible)</li>
  <li><strong>PHPMailer X-Mailer</strong> — used extensively in phishing kits; legitimate bank email uses enterprise mail infrastructure</li>
  <li><strong>Sending at 3:42 AM</strong> — automated bulk senders don't respect business hours</li>
  <li><strong>Urgency language</strong> — "URGENT", "compromised", "immediately" are classic social engineering pressure tactics</li>
</ul>

<h3>Identifying Homograph Attacks</h3>

<p>
  Unicode homograph attacks use characters from different alphabets that look identical to Latin characters. For example, the Cyrillic letter <strong>а</strong> (U+0430) looks identical to the Latin <strong>a</strong> (U+0061). An attacker could register <code>pаypal.com</code> where the "a" is Cyrillic — visually indistinguishable.
</p>

<p>To detect these:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Copy the suspicious domain and paste it into a Unicode inspector tool (like <code>unicode.tools</code>)</li>
  <li>Check the domain's TLD — most registrars now block mixed-script registrations, but historical domains may exist</li>
  <li>Look for the <code>xn--</code> Punycode encoding in the raw headers (e.g., <code>xn--pypal-4ve.com</code>)</li>
</ul>

<h3>Summary: The Investigator's Checklist</h3>

<div class="callout success">
  <span class="callout-title">✅ Quick Reference Checklist</span>
  <ol style="margin-top:10px; padding-left:18px;">
    <li>Read Received headers <strong>bottom-up</strong> to find the true origin IP</li>
    <li>Identify your mail gateway as the <strong>trust anchor</strong></li>
    <li>Check <strong>SPF, DKIM, DMARC</strong> results in <code>Authentication-Results</code></li>
    <li>Confirm <strong>From</strong> vs <strong>Return-Path</strong> domain alignment</li>
    <li>Run a <strong>WHOIS lookup</strong> on the originating IP</li>
    <li>Check the IP against <strong>blacklists</strong> (MXToolbox, Spamhaus)</li>
    <li>Run <strong>GeoIP</strong> lookup — note limitations (VPN, proxy)</li>
    <li>Inspect <strong>X-Mailer</strong> and <strong>Message-ID</strong> for phishing kit signatures</li>
    <li>Check for <strong>timestamp anomalies</strong> in the Received chain</li>
    <li>Verify the <strong>sending domain</strong> for look-alike or homograph spoofing</li>
  </ol>
</div>

